      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research an overhead-conscious parallel quicksort for the four-core ARM64 target. Keep case generation and its PRNG sequence unchanged; parallelize only after generation, using race-free pivot selection, independent partitions, and generic size/depth limits with a serial fallback. Validate the exact sorted-array MD5 and output.\",\"B\":\"Research a distinct parallel mergesort or chunk-sort/merge approach, using one reusable scratch buffer and bounded parallel work. Account for its extra memory and task overhead with a generic small-input fallback. Preserve generation and checksum behavior, and evaluate end-to-end rather than assuming sorting dominates.\",\"C\":\"Research a low-overhead, robust sequential alternative such as introsort: insertion sort for small ranges, a worst-case heapsort fallback, and a partition strategy that handles duplicates. Replace only sorting behavior; retain exact generated values, sortedness validation, and MD5 output. Compare full-run performance against the existing quicksort.\",\"G\":\"Individually validate candidate programs, then measure any proposed integration on the actual runner and official cases, including the required repeated timing samples. Confirm exact output/checksum correctness. Compare integrated end-to-end results with the baseline and each candidate; do not assume gains add, and reject combinations that measure worse. Keep generation and checksum unchanged.\",\"analysis\":\"The supplied baseline is valid: mean 0.068926 seconds (samples 0.068874–0.068967), score 0.5, and correctness on 4 cases. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPU. The source serially generates data, uses a shared custom PRNG for recursive two-way quicksort pivots, then checks sortedness and computes MD5. No phase timings or case sizes are supplied, so do not assume sorting dominates; the short runtime makes parallel overhead a particular risk. Use generic runtime heuristics, never special-case known inputs, and preserve correctness for every valid input.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"a83f3de7518899b60a2feeea573fb1041e5b5b8231eadb5de095e74dcb6f59f4\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research an overhead-conscious parallel quicksort for the four-core ARM64 target. Keep case generation and its PRNG sequence unchanged; parallelize only after generation, using race-free pivot selection, independent partitions, and generic size/depth limits with a serial fallback. Validate the exact sorted-array MD5 and output.\",\"B\":\"Research a distinct parallel mergesort or chunk-sort/merge approach, using one reusable scratch buffer and bounded parallel work. Account for its extra memory and task overhead with a generic small-input fallback. Preserve generation and checksum behavior, and evaluate end-to-end rather than assuming sorting dominates.\",\"C\":\"Research a low-overhead, robust sequential alternative such as introsort: insertion sort for small ranges, a worst-case heapsort fallback, and a partition strategy that handles duplicates. Replace only sorting behavior; retain exact generated values, sortedness validation, and MD5 output. Compare full-run performance against the existing quicksort.\",\"G\":\"Individually validate candidate programs, then measure any proposed integration on the actual runner and official cases, including the required repeated timing samples. Confirm exact output/checksum correctness. Compare integrated end-to-end results with the baseline and each candidate; do not assume gains add, and reject combinations that measure worse. Keep generation and checksum unchanged.\",\"analysis\":\"The supplied baseline is valid: mean 0.068926 seconds (samples 0.068874–0.068967), score 0.5, and correctness on 4 cases. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPU. The source serially generates data, uses a shared custom PRNG for recursive two-way quicksort pivots, then checks sortedness and computes MD5. No phase timings or case sizes are supplied, so do not assume sorting dominates; the short runtime makes parallel overhead a particular risk. Use generic runtime heuristics, never special-case known inputs, and preserve correctness for every valid input.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"b25bdc3f813f4979a2a3477ae6817975\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"a83f3de7518899b60a2feeea573fb1041e5b5b8231eadb5de095e74dcb6f59f4\",\"session_id\":\"c08531b8446a447bacb06c4500c0c033\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"d54b001b79bda936f33f0474dc7f2985097c180f99eb1926aad820a328ba4bf1\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":12398,\"output_tokens\":3482,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":3082}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":15880,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
