    {
      "id": 17,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"synthesis\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Build on the fastest reported candidate (36bf0f96368383910f1632aa16ea9fb0b8762f94, 0.028692 s): keep case generation and its PRNG sequence unchanged; explore the median-of-three, three-way quicksort with small-range insertion sort and coarse OpenMP tasks. Tune task and small-range cutoffs across the available cases for the 4-core ARM64 target, not for one size. Keep tasks on disjoint ranges, avoid shared PRNG use while sorting, and compare adding an introspective heapsort fallback for worst-case protection. Validate the sorted contents and checksum.\",\"B\":\"Move beyond the standalone verifier and recursion micro-optimizations: their reported means (0.068966 s and 0.069696 s) did not improve on the 0.068926 s baseline. Explore a separate coarse-grained parallel merge-sort implementation with one checked scratch allocation, a sequential fallback for small ranges or unavailable scratch, and correct signed-integer comparisons. Preserve generation, PRNG state, sorted-array contents, and MD5 behavior; evaluate total runtime across cases rather than assuming this alternative will beat quicksort.\",\"C\":\"Explore a distinct general-integer sorting approach: a four-pass byte-wise LSD radix sort with correct signed ordering (for example, biasing the sign bit), optional coarse OpenMP work, and a sequential fallback for small inputs or scratch-allocation failure. Do not assume values are nonnegative or specialize to generated test data. Preserve the generated sequence and verify exact sorted contents and MD5 output. Compare against the measured introsort candidate (0d0df70a75856be3cf8d1d840488fbe6e3ae1bd9, 0.028977 s); its near-best time is evidence to beat, not a guaranteed gain.\",\"G\":\"Integrate and measure whole candidates independently; do not stack A/B/C changes on the assumption that gains add. Use the reported A candidate (36bf0f96368383910f1632aa16ea9fb0b8762f94, 0.028692 s) as the leading measured comparator, and compare the merge-sort and radix-sort programs on equal conditions. Recheck correctness and checksum behavior on all available cases, then select by repeated end-to-end measurements; retain the simplest best verified program if an integration is slower. Keep the generator and PRNG sequence unchanged and do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, or target.\",\"analysis\":\"The official task is to sort the generated positive-integer array and emit the MD5 checksum, so preserving the source's deterministic generation sequence and exact sorted contents is essential. The correct baseline mean is 0.068926 s. The fastest reported candidate is A's three-way quicksort at 0.028692 s (about 2.4x the baseline); C's introsort is close at 0.028977 s, while another C candidate is 0.038310 s. B's verifier change is effectively baseline speed and its recursion change is slower; A's Hoare-partition candidate is also slower at 0.080943 s. These measurements motivate tuning the successful quicksort family and testing distinct general-purpose sort families, while requiring fresh correctness and timing checks because integrated results need not be additive.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"cc6a7d7efa7ab1633d4c6c3107df619cf653aab7763939a0e32661c6ddc3dbbe\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Build on the fastest reported candidate (36bf0f96368383910f1632aa16ea9fb0b8762f94, 0.028692 s): keep case generation and its PRNG sequence unchanged; explore the median-of-three, three-way quicksort with small-range insertion sort and coarse OpenMP tasks. Tune task and small-range cutoffs across the available cases for the 4-core ARM64 target, not for one size. Keep tasks on disjoint ranges, avoid shared PRNG use while sorting, and compare adding an introspective heapsort fallback for worst-case protection. Validate the sorted contents and checksum.\",\"B\":\"Move beyond the standalone verifier and recursion micro-optimizations: their reported means (0.068966 s and 0.069696 s) did not improve on the 0.068926 s baseline. Explore a separate coarse-grained parallel merge-sort implementation with one checked scratch allocation, a sequential fallback for small ranges or unavailable scratch, and correct signed-integer comparisons. Preserve generation, PRNG state, sorted-array contents, and MD5 behavior; evaluate total runtime across cases rather than assuming this alternative will beat quicksort.\",\"C\":\"Explore a distinct general-integer sorting approach: a four-pass byte-wise LSD radix sort with correct signed ordering (for example, biasing the sign bit), optional coarse OpenMP work, and a sequential fallback for small inputs or scratch-allocation failure. Do not assume values are nonnegative or specialize to generated test data. Preserve the generated sequence and verify exact sorted contents and MD5 output. Compare against the measured introsort candidate (0d0df70a75856be3cf8d1d840488fbe6e3ae1bd9, 0.028977 s); its near-best time is evidence to beat, not a guaranteed gain.\",\"G\":\"Integrate and measure whole candidates independently; do not stack A/B/C changes on the assumption that gains add. Use the reported A candidate (36bf0f96368383910f1632aa16ea9fb0b8762f94, 0.028692 s) as the leading measured comparator, and compare the merge-sort and radix-sort programs on equal conditions. Recheck correctness and checksum behavior on all available cases, then select by repeated end-to-end measurements; retain the simplest best verified program if an integration is slower. Keep the generator and PRNG sequence unchanged and do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, or target.\",\"analysis\":\"The official task is to sort the generated positive-integer array and emit the MD5 checksum, so preserving the source's deterministic generation sequence and exact sorted contents is essential. The correct baseline mean is 0.068926 s. The fastest reported candidate is A's three-way quicksort at 0.028692 s (about 2.4x the baseline); C's introsort is close at 0.028977 s, while another C candidate is 0.038310 s. B's verifier change is effectively baseline speed and its recursion change is slower; A's Hoare-partition candidate is also slower at 0.080943 s. These measurements motivate tuning the successful quicksort family and testing distinct general-purpose sort families, while requiring fresh correctness and timing checks because integrated results need not be additive.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"fa181f152bf7479387f63d9f11b72e73\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"cc6a7d7efa7ab1633d4c6c3107df619cf653aab7763939a0e32661c6ddc3dbbe\",\"session_id\":\"c08531b8446a447bacb06c4500c0c033\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"9a5d18bae08f4e48c393e9889f802be660865f91c44b3ef4f58524e36a20d197\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":24276,\"output_tokens\":6211,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":5506}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":30487,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
